Reset, instead of accepting, a TCP connection from the tun that keqdroid's
tun-ipv6 rule closes, so the app goes on to its IPv4 address.

On the desktop, with a global IPv6 address on the machine and "Keep IPv6
inside the tunnel" on, keqdroid routes ::/0 into the TUN and closes it with a
rule tagged tun-ipv6 that leads to a blackhole. The stack answers a SYN before
routing runs (proxy/tun/stack.go, and gVisor did so as well), so the app saw a
connection open and then close. Happy Eyeballs had already settled on IPv6 and
never tried IPv4: a browser on IPv6 (Chromium prefers it) opened no site at
all, while Telegram and other IPv4 apps worked (complaint of 19.11.2026,
Windows, mobile internet shared from a phone).

proxy/tun now applies that one rule before the handshake, with
Router.MatchRuleTag from xray-owner-guard.patch, and answers a match with a
reset, as a port nobody listens on would. The rule matches the address and
inbound tag only, so the check stays in the loop that reads the tun. No
tun-ipv6 rule in the config (Android, or IPv6 not taken into the TUN) means no
match and no change. UDP has no handshake and keeps being dropped by the rule
itself.

Applies after xray-owner-guard.patch (file names sort that way).

Built by tool/build_xray_cores.ps1. Checked against xray-core v26.11.9.

